[PASSAGE]
Every few years someone demonstrates that the queue is stupid, and the demonstration is correct. First come, first served allocates a scarce thing by who was able to be standing somewhere at seven in the morning, which is to say it rewards the unemployed, the local, the childless, the well, and the merely stubborn. Any rule that sorted by need would perform better on the very thing the queue is supposed to be for. So the reformers arrive with their points systems, their weighted lotteries, their triage matrices, and the arithmetic is on their side, and for a season the line is abolished.
Then the line comes back. Not because the administrators are fools, and not always because the reform was gamed, though it usually was.
What a needs-based rule does, and a queue does not, is issue an invitation. Every criterion of need is a proposition about who counts more, and a proposition is a thing that can be disputed — appealed, lobbied, litigated, coached for by anyone with a friend who has been through it before. The rule must be published, and once published it can be met: the applicant learns which box scores highest and finds the language that puts him in it. What sinks these systems is not that they weigh need badly. It is that they weigh at all, in public, on stated grounds, and stated grounds are the raw material of argument.
The queue's criterion is the only one available that says nothing whatever about the claimant. You were here first makes no assessment, honours no property, ranks no life against another. Because it contains no claim, it offers nothing to contest; because it offers nothing to contest, it cannot be argued with, and this — not fairness, which it does not possess — is what carries it past the door and keeps it there. The arbitrariness that the reformers correctly identify as its central defect is functioning, all the while, as its qualification. And the natural amendment, the one every sensible person proposes, that the queue should stand except in cases of real urgency, is not a modest improvement to the rule. It is a repeal of the property that made the rule survivable, since "real urgency" is a stated ground and the argument the queue was holding shut begins again at the word.
The position holds, and it should be stated without softening: the queue earns its place by refusing to give reasons, and it earns it against every rival that gives them. What it costs is likewise not softenable, and it is not paid by the institution, which sleeps well. It is paid, in full, by the person whose reasons were the best in the room — who had, and knew he had, the strongest case anyone in that hall could have made, and who stands exactly where his arrival time put him while the clerk, who could not have been argued with, calls the next number.
[QUESTIONS]

Q1. Which of the following best captures the passage's central claim?
(A) The queue survives because it makes no claim about the claimant and so admits no argument — a virtue the reformers misread as its defect, and one paid for by the neediest.
(B) Needs-based allocation systems fail in practice because applicants with access to advice and coaching learn to present themselves in whatever terms the published criteria happen to reward.
(C) The queue is, on inspection, fairer than its critics allow, since treating every claimant identically without regard to circumstance is what fairness has always meant.
(D) Since both the queue and needs-based allocation impose real costs on some claimants, the choice between them cannot be settled on principle and is best left to administrators.

Q2. The author's overall posture toward the queue is best described as:
(A) affirming it as the just allocation, its arbitrariness being no defect at all once properly understood.
(B) affirming its rationale while naming the specific price, which falls on the claimant with the strongest case.
(C) rejecting it, since a rule that rewards the merely available cannot be defended against a rule that tracks need.
(D) declining to choose, on the grounds that the reformers and the defenders of the line are each right about something the other misses.

Q3. What is the primary function of the third paragraph ("What a needs-based rule does…")?
(A) It concedes that needs-based systems weigh need inaccurately, preparing the case for the queue's superior accuracy.
(B) It identifies the property — public, stated grounds — that exposes needs-based rules to argument, which is the property the queue lacks.
(C) It shows that reform failures are attributable chiefly to gaming, a problem better system design could in principle correct.
(D) It grants the reformers their arithmetic while denying that arithmetic has any bearing on how institutions actually allocate scarce goods.

Q4. A hospital adopts strict first-come triage but adds one exception: patients in immediate danger of death are seen first. On the author's account, this exception is best described as:
(A) a sensible compromise, preserving the queue's immunity to argument in the ordinary run of cases while correcting it in the extreme ones.
(B) consistent with the author's position, since he objects to stated criteria only where they can be coached for, which dying patients cannot do.
(C) a repeal of the property the author identifies, since "immediate danger" is a stated ground and therefore a thing that can be argued into.
(D) irrelevant to the author's argument, which concerns administrative allocation rather than clinical decisions made under time pressure.

Q5. The passage's argument most depends on the unstated assumption that:
(A) allocation rules cannot survive institutionally if they are subject to sustained contestation over their stated grounds.
(B) claimants are, on the whole, dishonest, and will misrepresent their circumstances whenever a rule gives them an opening.
(C) the goods that queues distribute are ones no claimant needs more urgently than any other.
(D) an allocation rule is legitimate if, and only if, it declines to assess the worth of those it allocates to.

Q6. Which finding, if established, would most weaken the author's central claim?
(A) Queues are, in fact, gamed as thoroughly as points systems, by people who pay others to stand in them.
(B) Some needs-based systems are unpopular with claimants who feel judged by them.
(C) Reformers have generally underestimated how much the unemployed and the local benefit from first-come rules.
(D) Needs-based systems with published criteria have, across decades and jurisdictions, proved at least as durable as queues and no more contested.

[ANSWER KEY & ELIMINATION LOGIC]
Q1 — Correct answer: (A)
(A) CORRECT — the immunity-to-argument thesis (para 4) with its named cost (para 5).
(B) example_promotion — coaching and gaming appear in para 3 as one consequence of stated grounds; the thesis is the stated grounds themselves ("not that they weigh need badly… that they weigh at all").
(C) terminological_twin — twins "immune to argument" with "fair"; para 4 explicitly disowns fairness ("not fairness, which it does not possess").
(D) false_symmetry — converts an asymmetric argument into a balanced ledger and a shrug; para 5 delivers a verdict ("The position holds").
Q2 — Correct answer: (B)
(A) moderate_extremism — sounds like the author's position minus its edge, but erases the cost he insists is not softenable (para 5).
(B) CORRECT — affirmation plus the price, paid by the claimant with the best case (para 5).
(C) half_truth — para 1 does grant the reformers their arithmetic; paras 4–5 then hold against them.
(D) false_symmetry — no verdict is withheld; the essay ends with one.
Q3 — Correct answer: (B)
(A) adjacent_answer — the author does not claim the queue weighs need more accurately; he says it does not weigh at all, and grants that need-based rules do better on need (para 1).
(B) CORRECT — stated, published grounds are "the raw material of argument," the property the queue's criterion lacks.
(C) half_truth — gaming is mentioned and then explicitly set aside as not the root ("not always because the reform was gamed, though it usually was"; "not that they weigh need badly").
(D) scope_inflation — the essay never denies arithmetic's bearing; it grants the arithmetic and locates the failure elsewhere.
Q4 — Correct answer: (C)
(A) moderate_extremism — the most reasonable-sounding option on the page, and the one para 4 is built to close off: the exception "is not a modest improvement… It is a repeal."
(B) half_truth — coachability is a symptom in para 3, not the criterion; the objection is to stated grounds as such.
(C) CORRECT — "real urgency" is a stated ground, and the argument resumes at the word (para 4).
(D) level_confusion — treats the argument as domain-bound; its terms are about criteria and contestability, not about which sector applies them.
Q5 — Correct answer: (A)
(A) CORRECT — the whole survival story (paras 2–4, "carries it past the door and keeps it there") presupposes that contestable rules do not last.
(B) scope_inflation — the passage needs only that claimants can meet published criteria, not that they are generally dishonest.
(C) straw_version — the author asserts the opposite; para 5's cost depends on some claimant having genuinely the best case.
(D) nec_suff_swap — converts a claim about institutional survival into a biconditional about legitimacy, which the essay never makes; it says the queue is not fair, only durable.
Q6 — Correct answer: (D)
(A) adjacent_answer — gaming is not the mechanism the thesis rests on; the queue can be gamed and still admit no argument about worth.
(B) half_truth — being felt as judgmental is consistent with, and mildly supportive of, the author's account of stated grounds.
(C) level_confusion — bears on who benefits from the queue's arbitrariness, not on why it survives.
(D) CORRECT — if published criteria prove as durable and no more contested, the survival mechanism in paras 3–4 loses its explanatory work.
[BLUEPRINT NOTE] Structure: a rule whose acknowledged defect is identified as the source of its survival — arbitrariness as immunity to argument, with the "sensible exception" shown to be a repeal rather than an amendment | Posture: resolution_costed | Thesis visible: para 4 | Traps: false_symmetry (paras 1–2, reformers and queue as a balanced trade-off), moderate_extremism (para 4, the reasonable-sounding urgency exception)
